#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# factor_integer

#% text
# `factor_integer(n)` gives the prime factorization of the integer `n` as a list of pairs `[p, e]` of a prime and its exponent.

#% text
# `factor_integer(r)` gives the factorization of the rational number `r`, with negative exponents for the primes of the denominator.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The primes are in increasing order.

#% item
# A negative number has the pair `[-1, 1]` first.

#% item
# `factor_integer(1)` is `[[1, 1]]` and `factor_integer(0)` is `[[0, 1]]`.

#% item
# Large factors are found by Pollard's rho method and the elliptic curve method; a number with two very large prime factors can take a long time.

#% item
# Only exact integers and fractions are factored; any other argument stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
factor_integer(360)
#% output
#= [[2, 3], [3, 2], [5, 1]]

#% cell
factor_integer(17)
#% output
#= [[17, 1]]

#% text
# A negative number:

#% cell
factor_integer(-12)
#% output
#= [[-1, 1], [2, 2], [3, 1]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Fractions:

#% cell
factor_integer(3/8)
#% output
#= [[2, -3], [3, 1]]

#% cell
factor_integer(-45/14)
#% output
#= [[-1, 1], [2, -1], [3, 2], [5, 1], [7, -1]]

#% text
# Larger numbers:

#% cell
factor_integer(factorial(20))
#% output
#= [[2, 18], [3, 8], [5, 4], [7, 2], [11, 1], [13, 1], [17, 1], [19, 1]]

#% cell
factor_integer(10^20 + 1)
#% output
#= [[73, 1], [137, 1], [1676321, 1], [5964848081, 1]]

#% text
# The Fermat number 2^64 + 1 and the larger 2^128 + 1:

#% cell
factor_integer(2^64 + 1)
#% output
#= [[274177, 1], [67280421310721, 1]]

#% cell
factor_integer(2^128 + 1)
#% output
#= [[59649589127497217, 1], [5704689200685129054721, 1]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Multiplying the powers gives the number back:

#% cell
product(map(p => p[1]^p[2], factor_integer(360)))
#% output
#= 360

#% text
# The number of divisors is the product of the exponents plus one:

#% cell
product(map(p => p[2] + 1, factor_integer(360)))
#% output
#= 24

#% cell
length(divisors(360))
#% output
#= 24

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# `factor_integer` threads over a list, as `map` does: the result is a list of factorizations, one level deeper than a single one:

#% cell
factor_integer([12, 15])
#% output
#= [[[2, 2], [3, 1]], [[3, 1], [5, 1]]]

#% cell
map(factor_integer, [12, 15])
#% output
#= [[[2, 2], [3, 1]], [[3, 1], [5, 1]]]

#% text
# A machine real is not factored:

#% cell
factor_integer(2.0)
#% output
#= factor_integer(2.0)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `is_prime`, `divisors`, `prime`, `gcd`, `euler_phi`
